      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village is surrounded on all sides by numerous ranches, including Philmont Scout Ranch, an extensive \"high‑adventure\" base operated by Scouting America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cimarron%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Once home to Anasazi, Jicarilla Apache, and Ute Indians, Cimarron on the Mountain Branch of the Santa Fe Trail became a hub of mining and ranching.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cimarron%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cimarron was the county seat of Colfax County beginning in 1872, when it replaced Elizabethtown, but the seat moved to Springer in 1881.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cimarron%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cimarron sits on the Cimarron River, a tributary of the 900‑mile‑long Canadian River, whose headwaters are at the Eagle Nest Dam.",
